      "text": "For a polynomial to have integer roots, those roots must be factors of the constant integer part (i.e. $7$). Thus, for a polynomial of the form \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\), an integer root can be \\((1), (-1), (1), -(1), (2), (-2), (4), (-4), (7), (-7), (8), (-8), \\dots\\)\n(Use multisets of roots.) Substituting in each of these roots and solving for $a$, we find:\r\n\r\nWhen $x=1$: $1 + 3 + a + 7 = 0 \\implies a = -11$.\r\nWhen $x=-1$: $-1 + 3 - a + 7 = 0 \\implies a = 9$.\r\nWhen $x=2$: $8 + 12 + 2a + 7 = 0 \\implies a = -23$.\r\nWhen $x=-2$: $-8+12-2a+7 = 0 \\implies a = 9$.\r\nWhen $x=4$: $64 + 48 + 4a + 7 = 0 \\implies a = -51$.\r\nWhen $x=-4$: $-64+48-4a+7 = 0 \\implies a = 39$.\r\nWhen $x=7$: $343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 0 \\implies a = -61$.\r\nWhen $x=-7$: $-343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = 0 \\implies a = 163$.\r\nWhen $x=8$: $512 + 192 + 8a + 7 = 0 \\implies a = -96$.\r\nWhen $x=-8$: $-512+192-8a+7 = 0 \\implies a = 41$.\r\nWhen $x=9$: $729+243+9a+7 = 0 \\implies a = -888$.\r\nWhen $x=1/2$: $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0$.\r\nThus, our answers are \\(\\boxed{-11, 9, -23, 9, -51, 39, -61, 163, 96, -888, 50}\\). The answer is -11,9,-23,9,-51,39,-61,163,96,-888,50",
